# The web server's TLS key that the releases shipped (FINDINGS 132). Every OpenWrt and OpenWrt-LuCI image from
# v2026.10.13 to v2026.10.16.1 carried /etc/uhttpd.key, one key per image and the same on every device that installed
# it; anyone who downloads the release has that key, so LuCI over HTTPS could be impersonated. The image build no
# longer puts one in (build-rootfs.sh). This takes a shipped key away on the devices: a key file that is byte for byte
# one of them goes, with its certificate, and uhttpd makes the device its own pair when it starts (it does whenever
# the pair is missing; px5g, an EC P-256 key). A key that is not on the list - one the device made, one the user put
# there - is never touched.
#
# uci-defaults run at boot before uhttpd starts, and the image brings this file again with every update (mu300-update,
# the Magisk zip, the computer's installer: each carries /etc over, and a shipped key with it where the old image is
# not known), so the check runs on the first boot of each image; it removes itself when it ran, as uci-defaults do.
# By hand on a running system (the file is gone after the first boot: take it from the repository):
# "sh 92-mu300-uhttpd-key; /etc/init.d/uhttpd restart". The browser warns once about the new self-signed certificate;
# LuCI over plain HTTP is not affected.
#
# The list: sha256 of etc/uhttpd.key in each published mu300-openwrt-rootfs.tar.gz and mu300-openwrt-luci-rootfs.tar.gz
